// The external-library half of the TestsInitializer leak: an @Test in an external
// (-Xauto-cache-from) klib registers and runs in the consumer's IC test binary. The plain
// -l per-file-cache half is already pinned by the corpus case
// ungrouped/dependencyKlibTestsRunInConsumerTestBinary (same root cause: the second-phase
// TestsInitializer lowering is registered unconditionally).
//
// getLoweringsAfterInlining (NativeLoweringPhases.kt:109) registers ::TestsInitializer for
// every compilation -- unlike the adjacent ::TestsDumper.takeIf { generateTestDumper } --
// and the IC CacheBuilder deserializes and lowers every file of every classpath klib,
// including the monolithic auto-cache it builds for an -Xauto-cache-from klib. The external
// library's klib carries the deliberately-dead private createTestSuites registration
// function annotated @kotlin.native.internal.TestInitializer (serialized by the first-phase
// TestProcessor per the KT-74223 two-phase design), and TestsInitializer bakes it into an
// eagerly-initialized @ThreadLocal field inside the cache bitcode (nm shows the
// createTestSuites$$FUNCTION_REFERENCE_FOR$extTest symbols in libexternalLib-cache.a). The
// consumer's -generate-test-runner binary then runs that field's initializer at startup and
// registers the external module's suite.
//
// So every IC link -- including from a completely fresh cache (dirty=0: the defect is baked
// at cache-build time) -- runs 2 tests and the external module's extTest fails the binary
// (exit 1), while a non-IC build of the same klibs never materializes the private
// registration function, runs 1 test and passes. Per the KT-74223 contract only files of
// modules being linked may turn the serialized registration into a running initializer, so
// a dependency klib's @Test must not run in the consumer's test binary: the assertEquals
// values below match the correct (non-IC) build, the test fails today and passes after a
// fix.

MODULES: externalLib, main

STEP 0:
    libs: externalLib, main
